---

<Docs title="Cast">
  <div class="hero">
    <h1>Build, test, and deploy AI agents.</h1>
    <p>
      Cast is a CLI and terminal UI for creating, configuring, and shipping
      production-ready AI agents. Describe what your agent should do; Cast
      scaffolds the agent, wires up tools and permissions, and deploys it
      to your platform of choice.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="badge accent" href="getting-started/install">Install</a>
      <a class="badge" href="getting-started/quickstart">Quick start</a>
      <a class="badge" href="cli/reference">CLI reference</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <ManifestBadge />

  <h2>What you can do with Cast</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href="agents/create" style="text-decoration:none;color:inherit;">
      <h3>Create an agent</h3>
      <p><code>cast new</code> launches an interactive wizard that scaffolds a new agent with instructions, tools, and a model.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="versioning/overview" style="text-decoration:none;color:inherit;">
      <h3>Version changes and releases</h3>
      <p>Automatic <em>change</em> snapshots for debugging, intentional <em>release</em> tags for shipping.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="deployment/overview" style="text-decoration:none;color:inherit;">
      <h3>Deploy anywhere</h3>
      <p>Railway, Fly.io, DigitalOcean, Sprites, Docker, or local. Each agent gets a unique deployment SHA so you can run many in parallel.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="tui/overview" style="text-decoration:none;color:inherit;">
      <h3>Chat in your terminal</h3>
      <p>Streaming responses, slash commands for release and deploy workflows, plus an on-host TUI that runs inside the container.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>How Cast is organized</h2>
  <p>This site is auto-generated where it should be and hand-written where it matters.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reference</strong> — every <code>cast &lt;command&gt;</code> and <code>/slash-command</code> is extracted from the source on every build.</li>
    <li><strong>Prose</strong> — concept pages, guides, and deployment deep-dives are written by humans and linked from the reference.</li>
    <li><strong>Status</strong> — see when the reference was last extracted, what's new in the manifest, and which commands still need a prose page.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
